{"path":"outputs/review_replies/thread_1.md","content":"# Thread 5 — dead guard in `_nonQueueRetryable` (fallback_stream.dart)

Applied — kept the guard as an explicit defensive precondition and reworded the comment to describe **this line**, not the caller (`lib/src/model_roles/fallback_stream.dart`, `_nonQueueRetryable`):

```dart
_Retryable? _nonQueueRetryable(ErrorEvent event) {
  // Precondition: the caller routes only StopReason.error here — a
  // non-error pre-commit error event (a user/machine abort) stands
  // verbatim via the forward-on-null path in [_forwardOrRetryable].
  assert(event.reason == StopReason.error);
```

Why keep it rather than drop it: dropping the guard removes the only safety net if a future caller ever routes abort-class events here (the queue-mode sibling in `_forwardOrRetryable` handles aborts by returning `null` too, but through the classifier's own guard); the `assert` documents the precondition AND enforces it in dev/test runs at zero production cost.

Behavior-identical: in production (`--enable-asserts` stripped) the branch set is unchanged — the guard was unreachable from the single call site either way. Pinned by the existing `aborted streams are forwarded without retry` test in `test/model_roles/fallback_stream_test.dart` (still green), and `dart analyze` is clean. The same-shape guards in `classifyQueueDeath` (public classifier, own contract) and `_retryableWireFailure` (three call sites) are live code, not this pattern — left untouched.
